---
import FrameworkLayout from '../../layouts/FrameworkLayout.astro';

const sections = ['Overview', 'The Five Forces', 'How to Apply', 'Real-World Example', 'Key Takeaways'];
---

<FrameworkLayout title="Porter's Five Forces" sections={sections}>
  <h1 id="overview">Porter's Five Forces</h1>
  
  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Porter's Five Forces is a foundational framework for understanding the competitive dynamics within an industry. Developed by Michael Porter, it helps executives analyze the attractiveness and profitability of an industry by examining five key competitive forces.
  </p>
  <p>
    This framework is essential for strategic planning, market entry decisions, and competitive positioning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-five-forces">The Five Forces</h2>
  
  <h3>1. Threat of New Entrants</h3>
  <p>
    How easily can new competitors enter the market? Barriers to entry such as capital requirements, economies of scale, brand loyalty, and regulatory requirements determine this force's strength.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Bargaining Power of Suppliers</h3>
  <p>
    How much influence do suppliers have over your business? Few suppliers, differentiated products, and high switching costs increase supplier power and can squeeze margins.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Bargaining Power of Buyers</h3>
  <p>
    How much influence do customers have? High buyer power emerges when customers are concentrated, products are undifferentiated, or switching costs are low.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Threat of Substitutes</h3>
  <p>
    What alternative solutions exist for customers? Substitutes can come from different industries and may meet customer needs in novel ways, limiting pricing power.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Competitive Rivalry</h3>
  <p>
    How intense is competition among existing players? Fast growth, high fixed costs, low product differentiation, and numerous competitors all increase rivalry intensity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-apply">How to Apply</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Assess each of the five forces on a scale of weak to strong</li>
    <li>Identify which forces have the greatest impact on profitability</li>
    <li>Determine how each force might change over time</li>
    <li>Develop strategies to mitigate threats and capitalize on opportunities</li>
    <li>Monitor competitive dynamics regularly</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="real-world-example">Real-World Example</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Smartphone Industry:</strong> Apple faces intense rivalry but maintains pricing power through strong brand differentiation. Threat of substitutes is moderate (tablets, wearables), while high barriers to entry in manufacturing protect against new entrants. Supplier power is managed through vertical integration and supplier diversity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="key-takeaways">Key Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Five Forces reveals industry attractiveness and profit potential</li>
    <li>Different forces matter in different industries</li>
    <li>Use it to inform competitive strategy and positioning</li>
    <li>Revisit regularly as industry dynamics evolve</li>
    <li>Combine with other frameworks for comprehensive analysis</li>
  </ul>
</FrameworkLayout>
